\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand shows Asia needs banking upgrade - experts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, March 7 (Reuter) - Thailand's banking sector crisis brought repeated calls for an upgrade of Asia's immature financial infrastructure at an International Monetary Fund-Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Asia needs a stronger, more dynamic financial infrastructure," IMF managing director Michel Camdessus told the conference on financial integration in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The region's banking system has not kept pace with its economic development, and the speedy pace of economic reform has created an urgent need for an overhaul, delegates said. \par \par \uc2  "In many countries, in Asia and elsewhere, prudential regulation and supervision have not kept pace with the new complexities of the banking business," Camdessus said. \par \par \uc2  "If left unaddressed, this gap could pose dangers for domestic and external stability. Indeed, all countries must be vigilant about the strength of their banking systems, so that the monetary authorities can tighten policy when needed, without fear of aggravating banking sector problems." \par \par \uc2  Asset quality problems forced \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week to demand better provisioning by its banks. Thailand was hampered in dealing with the situation by its monetary policy. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the baht, is pegged to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated by the dollar, but was under pressure from speculators who were encouraged by Thailand's poor economic picture and weak financial sector. \par \par \uc2  Lower rates would have eased liquidity -- but would also have lowered the cost of borrowing for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  Higher interest rates to ward off the speculators would have increased the cost of borrowing for the cash-strapped banks. \par \par \uc2  While most speakers stopped short of calling for regional banking regulation and supervision to prevent the same situation occurring elsewhere -- an idea mooted by Australia recently -- some experts recommended frequent and regular gatherings of central bankers and finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  "Most countries need to take steps to strengthen the official supervisory oversight of financial institutions and markets," said Gerald Corrigan, managing director at Goldman Sachs and a former president of the New York and member of the Federal Open Market Committee. \par \par \uc2  Key to the debate are capital inflows, which have widened current account deficits across Asia with serious implications for external balances and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Corrigan warned that while many Asian nations with wide current account deficits have taken comfort from large \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the capital inflows responsible for much of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  can evaporate overnight. \par \par \uc2  "Deregulation has also led to a greater exposure of credit and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, and increased the danger of banks being inadequately capitalised with consequent incentives to offer above-market interest rates and engage in less prudent lending," said Mahmood Pradhan, a senior economist with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  The situation is becoming more urgent as more and more nations in the region seek financing for the sort of huge infrastructure projects on which their future depends, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The challenge is for many of these countries to address their infrastructure needs, including environmental requirements, without putting undue strains on public finances or on external positions," Pradhan said. \par \par \uc2  Although many Asian nations have successfully raised capital through privatisations, the amount of capital raised through privatisation in Asia was relatively small compared with Latin America, he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt3708xwa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
