\par\par \b \uc2  =Thailand/Charoen -3:Liquor Liberalization To Hit Empire \b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 1997\par 02:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Industry executives reckon that the retail market for dark and 'white' distilled spirits is at least 50 billion baht, but the market for the products made by Charoen's distilleries has plateaued in recent years, as beer and imported whiskey have increased in popularity among Thais. \par \par \uc2  And Charoen may not dominate that market for much longer. Thailand's bureaucracy and past administrations have pledged to liberalize the liquor industry when the concessions end in 1999, making it likely that Charoen will lose at least part of his liquor empire. \par \par \uc2  Still, many believe that his grip on the distilling industry is so strong that he stands a good chance of retaining a significant portion of the business. \par \par \uc2  Some industry executives speculate, for example, that Charoen may try to persuade the Thai government now under another long-time Charoen pal, Prime Minister Chavalit Yongchaiyudh to renew his monopoly to distill cheap but potent white spirits by arguing that opening that market could lead to abuses by less-experienced distillers. \par \par \uc2  Charoen's concessions now give his group 100% of the white liquor market and about 70% of the brown, or dark, liquor market. \par \par \uc2  Charoen's nonliquor investments have occasionally stirred controversy as well. Take his forays into banking, for example. In 1986, with the struggle over liquor concessions still unresolved, Charoen surprised Bangkok's financial community by buying into First Bangkok City Bank, or FBCB. \par \par \uc2  The bank was struggling with bad property loans, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses and management irregularities that eventually led the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a financial rescue package. Despite his big liquor debts at the time, Charoen won Finance Ministry approval to begin buying FBCB stock from the bank's key shareholder at a heavily discounted price just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Charoen and his associates have since accumulated about 35% of FBCB's equity, taking control of the bank, now Thailand's seventh largest, with assets of about $8.7 billion at the end of 1995. \par \par \uc2  FBCB, which is listed on the Stock Exchange of Thailand, is profitable, with annual earnings growth averaging almost 30% from 1993 through 1996. But the bank isn't known for innovation its loan, deposit and trade-finance businesses have been slow to automate. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES ASIAN EQUITIES REPORT 28-01-97 \par \par \uc2  0102GMT \par \par \uc2  1122 62542 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011004dt1s00bqm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
