\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand Baht Up In Early Trade On Fin. Min Remarks \b0\par\par\uc2 266 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 1996\par 03:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--The Thai baht is climbing against the dollar in early spot trading Wednesday, with investors snapping up the baht on remarks by the country's finance ministry that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 'undervalued.' \par \par \uc2  As a result, the baht is also perking up in the swap market, where investors make agreements to buy and sell dollars at specified times in the future. Swap premiums dropped. \par \par \uc2  Around 0230 GMT, the dollar is at 25.580 baht in the spot market, down from 25.588 baht at the close of trading Tuesday. The Bank of Thailand, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , fixed its midrate for the dollar at 25.55 baht, unchanged from Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said foreign investors sold the dollar in favor of the baht, following remarks by Finance Minister Amnuay Viravan that he favors the stable baht and that the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 'undervalued.' Amnuay made his remarks at a conference on Tuesday, during which he unveiled what he dubbed the 'action plan' to heal the country's ailing economy and stabilize the financial system. \par \par \uc2  'Amnuay's comment was positive for the baht; investors began selling the dollar following his remark,' said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Bangkok. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they believe the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also benefited from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in domestic swap market and in Singapore, which they estimate to be around $750 million. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank