\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand Baht -2: Exacerbate Worries On Economy \b0\par\par\uc2 296 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 1996\par 05:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investors' move to depart from the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Friday added to a series of sell-offs this week, reflecting their nervousness about the baht. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. attack on Iraq, the downgrade of Thailand's short-term foreign debt rating, and the rumors - which broke out in the stock market Thursday afternoon - helped exacerbate worries about the Thai economy. \par \par \uc2  Dollar-buying affected money supply in the interbank market, which has otherwise been expanding on the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  This week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has injected at least 25 billion baht into the repurchase market and bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  worth $105 million to prop up the baht and bring down the interbank rates. Its effort paid off Wednesday when the interbank rates dropped to 12%. But the interbank rates rose again early Friday as banks traded the baht for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate was at 11.25%-12%, up from 10%-11% late Thursday. The call rate was at 13%, up from 10.5%-10.75% Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Swap rates are as follows, in satang. (100 satangs = 1 baht).\par       Friday      Thursday    Wednesday   Tuesday\par 1 mo  9.25-10.25  6.0-7.0     10.0-10.75  10.75-11.75\par 2 mo  18.75-20.75 14.0-15.5   18.0-19.0   17.0-18.5\par 3 mo  26.25-28.25 19.5-20.5   25.5-27.0   24.5-26.0\par 6 mo  47.0-50.0   38.0-40.0   46.0-48.0   45.5-47.5\par 1 yr  84.0-88.0   76.0-78.0   84.0-88.0   84.0-86.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END)