\par\par \b \uc2  Thailand Baht Drops In Asian Trading On Panic-Selling \b0\par\par\uc2 299 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 1996\par 13:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BANGKOK (AP-Dow Jones)--The Thai baht slumped against the dollar in late Asian spot trading Thursday, stung by panic-selling on rumors that a handful of small securities companies were on the brink of collapse, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also weakened in the swap market, where investors make agreements to buy and sell baht at specified times in the future. Swap premiums rose from Wednesday's levels. \par \par \uc2  At the close of spot trading Thursday, the dollar was 25.324 baht in the spot market, up from 25.296 baht at the close of trading Wednesday. The Bank of Thailand, meanwhile, fixed its mid-rate for the dollar at 25.31 baht, down from 25.32 baht Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the baht's decline to late-afternoon rumors that about four finance and securities companies were about to collapse. The Securities and Exchange Commission denied the rumors, but the fright they generated spread from the stock market to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the sell-off reflected the high state of alarm among investors in Thailand. The country's export growth has slowed to a crawl, and the economy has begun to sputter. The U.S. attack on Iraq this week exacerbated worries in Thailand, a big oil importer. Meanwhile Moody's Investors Service downgraded the country's short-term foreign sovereign debt. \par \par \uc2  A sustained sell-off this week has prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Thailand\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly to prop up the baht and ease a tight money supply. On Tuesday and Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected about 25 billion baht into the repurchase market and bought dollars worth $110 million from Thai commercial banks at below-market rates. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 05-09-96 \par \par \uc2  1135GMT \par \par \uc2  1028 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds95075an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
